### What makes this question different from a standard incident question?

This question explicitly asks you to explain why the senior framing works. You must not only tell the story but articulate the meta-principle: evidence, impact, and prevention are what elevate a story from a timeline to a senior answer.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class SeniorVsJunior {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Junior: 'The service was down. We restarted it. It worked.'");
        System.out.println("Senior: 'Trade settlement was down 12min during market close (impact),");
        System.out.println("  I traced via thread dumps to a stalled FX dependency (evidence),");
        System.out.println("  and I added bulkhead isolation so one dependency cannot starve the pool (prevention).'");
    }
}
```

### How do you quantify impact in the Situation?

A junior says "the service was down." A senior quantifies: "the trade-settlement service was down for twelve minutes during market close, blocking settlement of about two thousand trades worth crores."

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class ImpactQuantification {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(ImpactQuantification.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int downtimeMinutes = 12;
        int tradesBlocked = 2000;
        String value = "crores";
        log.severe(() -> "Impact: " + downtimeMinutes + "min downtime, "
            + tradesBlocked + " trades worth " + value + " blocked");
    }
}
```

### How do you state specific ownership in the Task?

Not "the team fixed it" but "I was the incident commander and owned the resolution and communication."

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class SpecificOwnership {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Weak: 'The team handled it'");
        System.out.println("Strong: 'I was the incident commander. I owned resolution and communication.'");
    }
}
```

### How do you show evidence-based investigation in the Action?

Name the exact signals: a trace showing one downstream call taking seconds isolates the culprit. A thread dump with many threads WAITING on the same monitor reveals a lock or dependency stall.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DiagnosticEvidence {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(DiagnosticEvidence.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Trace: FX pricing call = 8000ms (all others < 50ms)");
        log.info("Thread dump: 40 threads WAITING on FxPricingClient.getRate()");
        log.info("Metric: thread pool = 40/40 (exhausted by one dependency)");
        log.info("Root cause: FX dependency stall starved the shared thread pool");
    }
}
```

### How do you show prevention in the Result?

A junior ends at "we restarted it and it worked." A senior ends with the permanent fix: "I introduced bulkhead isolation so one slow dependency cannot starve the whole service."

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class BulkheadIsolation {
    // Each dependency gets its own bounded pool
    private static final ExecutorService fxPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
    private static final ExecutorService mainPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(35);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("FX pool: 5 threads (bounded, cannot starve main)");
        System.out.println("Main pool: 35 threads (protected from FX stalls)");
        System.out.println("If FX stalls, only 5 threads are affected, not 40.");
    }
}
```

```text
FX pool: 5 threads (bounded, cannot starve main)
Main pool: 35 threads (protected from FX stalls)
If FX stalls, only 5 threads are affected, not 40.
```

### Why do evidence, impact, and prevention matter together?

Evidence shows you diagnose rather than guess. Impact shows you think in business terms. Prevention shows you make the system permanently better. Together they prove you can be trusted with the next incident.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class ThreeAnchors {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Evidence:   I diagnose from data, not guesses");
        System.out.println("Impact:     I think in business terms, not just technical");
        System.out.println("Prevention: I make the system permanently better");
        System.out.println("Together:   I can be trusted with the next incident");
    }
}
```

### How does communication cadence show seniority?

Regular, clear status updates during the incident are as important as the technical fix. A junior often omits communication entirely.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CommsCadence {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Every 5 minutes during incident:");
        System.out.println("  [14:00] Detected: settlement down, 2000 trades blocked");
        System.out.println("  [14:05] Investigating: FX dependency suspected");
        System.out.println("  [14:10] Confirmed: FX stall starving thread pool");
        System.out.println("  [14:12] Resolved: failed fast on FX, pool recovered");
    }
}
```

### What is the lasting takeaway about senior incident answers?

The three anchors : evidence, impact, prevention : are a universal framework. Use them for any incident story in any interview.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class IncidentFramework {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Framework for any incident STAR:");
        System.out.println("  Situation: quantify IMPACT");
        System.out.println("  Task: state specific OWNERSHIP");
        System.out.println("  Action: show EVIDENCE-based diagnosis");
        System.out.println("  Result: measurable recovery + PREVENTION");
    }
}
```
